S=['People are central to Good Manufacturing Practice. Every person whose activities can affect product quality needs to understand their assigned responsibilities. Clear reporting lines, appropriate staffing, and suitable supervision support consistent execution. Staff must follow approved procedures, record work accurately, and report abnormal conditions. GMP is not only the responsibility of the quality department; it depends on coordinated action across the site.', 'Personnel should receive initial and continuing training relevant to their duties. Training may include GMP principles, job-specific procedures, hygiene, safety and contamination control. The organization should evaluate whether personnel can perform assigned tasks competently, using methods appropriate to the task and risk. A training attendance record alone does not necessarily demonstrate practical competence. Retraining may be needed when procedures change or performance gaps are identified.', 'Personal hygiene helps reduce the risk of contamination. Personnel should follow site rules for health reporting, hand hygiene, protective clothing, and restrictions on items brought into manufacturing areas. Hygiene expectations depend on the activity and contamination risk. For example, personnel handling exposed product require controls appropriate to the manufacturing environment. Site procedures should clearly explain what to do when a hygiene concern is identified.', 'Gowning is a controlled process, not simply putting on a garment. The clothing type, gowning sequence, changing-room arrangements and entry controls depend on the area classification and operations. Personnel should be trained in the approved gowning procedure and any applicable qualification requirements. In sterile manufacturing, gowning controls are particularly important. This introductory lesson does not replace detailed aseptic gowning qualification or Annex One training.', 'Personnel behaviour can affect contamination control even when equipment and rooms are qualified. Follow approved movement restrictions, material flows and entry rules. Avoid unnecessary activity and interactions that could disturb controlled conditions. Do not touch exposed product-contact surfaces without appropriate controls. If an unexpected event occurs, stop or secure the activity as required and follow the site escalation procedure. Good habits help sustain a controlled state.', 'Contamination and mix-up risks can arise from incorrect materials, poor segregation, inappropriate handling, and unclear identification. Personnel should verify material and equipment status before use, follow line-clearance procedures where applicable, and respect area and campaign controls. Labels and records should support identification and traceability. When an item or status is uncertain, do not guess; seek clarification and document the concern according to procedure.', 'Supervisors should ensure that personnel understand their tasks and that required controls are followed. GMP records should identify who performed or checked an activity when required. Entries must reflect the actual work and should not be backdated or fabricated. Observation, coaching and periodic review can help identify training needs. A culture that supports timely reporting of mistakes and near misses contributes to product quality and patient protection.', 'Imagine an operator entering a controlled manufacturing area who notices that the required gowning step was missed. The appropriate response is not to continue silently. The operator should follow the site procedure, which may require stopping, leaving the area, regowning, and reporting the event. Any potential impact on operations or product quality must be assessed by authorized personnel. The key principle is to identify, contain, document and address the issue rather than conceal it.']
topics=[[(h,b)]*3 for h,b in [('RESPONSIBILITY', 'Everyone contributes to product quality'), ('TRAINING', 'Training plus task-appropriate competence'), ('HYGIENE', 'Hygiene matched to contamination risk'), ('GOWNING', 'Area-specific gowning and entry controls'), ('BEHAVIOUR', 'Disciplined behaviour in controlled areas'), ('SEGREGATION', 'Identify materials and prevent mix-ups'), ('RECORDS', 'Accurate records and effective supervision'), ('ESCALATION', 'Stop, report and assess missed controls')]]
